WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.981
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:08.330
I want to tell you about
three of my friends.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.250
First there is Laura.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:13.426
She loves astrology.

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:15.189
She's a Scorpio.

00:00:15.189 --> 00:00:16.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.370
reality TV, especially the show Survivor.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.298
Once when she knew I was sad,
she mailed me four pints of my

00:00:25.298 --> 00:00:30.129
favorite ice cream across the country,
overnight in dry ice.

00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:35.166
Then there's Joanna,

00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:40.540
a fellow engineer who loves to whine
with me about the worst parts of work.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.250
When we went to Arizona together,
she became so concerned that I would get

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.456
dehydrated that she bribed me to finish
my water bottle with a bag of doughnuts.

00:00:52.456 --> 00:00:58.436
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And then, there's Arthur,

00:00:58.436 --> 00:01:02.972
a late night philosopher who was always
the first person to brave the cold in

00:01:02.972 --> 00:01:07.802
Boston when we had all been out for a late
night of drinking and needed some food.

00:01:10.484 --> 00:01:13.410
We all have these people in our lives.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.579
But how do you know who
your friends really are?

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.280
For me, it was trauma that made
the lines in my community really clear.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.600
Two of these people are still
among my best friends.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:34.827
And the third, not someone I
choose to interact with any more.

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:41.799
So about that trauma,
it was the summer between my sophomore and

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:43.460
junior year of college.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.155
And we were celebrating graduation
when a classmate raped me.

00:01:50.462 --> 00:01:53.330
But this talk is not really about him or
that night.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.717
It's about everyone else and
what happened afterwards.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.532
It's about you.

00:02:06.958 --> 00:02:10.960
When I first started telling people
about what happened, I was scared.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.120
He was friends with everybody.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.469
So why would they believe me?

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:21.170
But I quickly found that
actually everybody believed me.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.050
In fact, they were even really surprised.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:33.350
I got a lot of euphemisms in response like
I knew he had had some issues with women.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:38.682
And one person told me that
his freshman year of college,

00:02:38.682 --> 00:02:42.839
he had broken his hand twice,
punching walls while drinking.

00:02:45.611 --> 00:02:48.666
On some level, they all already knew.

00:02:51.979 --> 00:02:53.667
We all have these people
in our communities as well.

00:02:53.667 --> 00:02:58.008
So why do we keep
inviting them to parties?

00:03:01.541 --> 00:03:06.090
And what did my three friends do
in the aftermath once they knew?

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.210
Well, a couple years later,
I was walking to work, and

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:15.070
I saw an article in a new site online
about my college dorm community.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.511
It prominently quoted my rapist talking
about how he felt free to be himself

00:03:21.511 --> 00:03:22.667
in this culture.

00:03:25.693 --> 00:03:30.569
This article was glowingly
reposted by many people who knew

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:33.115
including Arthur and Joanna.

00:03:36.172 --> 00:03:41.100
I used to tell people I wasn't trying to
get them to pick a side, his versus mine.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:49.640
It felt like the polite fair answer, one
that allows for complexity and redemption.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.300
As if this was just a bad breakup and
I'm the one who's already moved on.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.920
But it is not that and
I know what I really want, for none of

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.410
my friends to talk to him ever again or
elevate his thoughts on social media.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.320
I don't want to see his company
on my college's website.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:18.040
I want it to be illegal for well-meaning
progressives to post diatribes

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.680
online decrying a culture that
allows Brett Kavanaugh to prosper.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:29.083
Even as they remain friendly and
cordial with the Brett Kavanaughs in

00:04:29.083 --> 00:04:35.324
their own community Whenever
a rapist is in the news,

00:04:35.324 --> 00:04:40.180
whether it's mine or Ben Roethlisberger,
I want rape in the headline.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:42.717
For how long?

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:46.390
Maybe forever.

00:04:48.514 --> 00:04:50.439
Maybe just until I stop feeling angry.

00:04:52.931 --> 00:04:54.894
Definitely at least a little bit longer.

00:04:58.714 --> 00:05:03.288
Arthur deleted his post after I publicly
commented on his support of a rapist.

00:05:03.288 --> 00:05:08.049
He briefly apologized and
then went on to do something very similar,

00:05:08.049 --> 00:05:10.030
only a couple months later.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.148
We do not talk anymore.

00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:21.390
Joanna deleted her post as well,
as I told her how betrayed I felt.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:27.519
She asked how could she be forgiven for
something that felt unforgivable to me?

00:05:29.848 --> 00:05:33.810
She would ask again,
crying that night in my apartment.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.336
And again a month or two later,

00:05:35.336 --> 00:05:38.757
as she told me all the things she
loved the most about our friendship.

00:05:41.144 --> 00:05:44.354
I didn't really have an answer for
her then,

00:05:44.354 --> 00:05:48.168
only that I hoped things
would be okay again someday.

00:05:51.423 --> 00:05:54.056
Not everyone gets a second chance,
but then again, so

00:05:54.056 --> 00:05:55.840
many people don't even ask for one.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.301
They're not willing to live
with the discomfort of

00:06:00.301 --> 00:06:04.822
knowing that they hurt someone they cared
about long enough to make it right.

00:06:08.104 --> 00:06:13.985
Meanwhile, Laura, in true Scorpio fashion,
went on what she calls a rampage.

00:06:13.985 --> 00:06:17.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She took to Twitter

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:18.780
under her public profile and

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:23.205
real name, not her burner account where
she posts her hot takes about Survivor.

00:06:23.205 --> 00:06:27.144
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And shredded the article,

00:06:27.144 --> 00:06:32.224
the people reposting it, and the community
norms that make it all possible.

00:06:35.396 --> 00:06:40.458
Years later, when I'm feeling low,
I still look for that tweet

00:06:40.458 --> 00:06:46.390
because I want people to pick my side,
even when it doesn't come for free.

00:06:51.693 --> 00:06:56.890
When I was writing this talk, I wanted
to give you a process or a framework.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.910
I'm an engineer and a pilot and
I like structure, checklists, fail safes.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:06.950
A technical fix will keep us safe.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.750
Because the alternative is what?

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.740
I tell you how angry I am?

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.029
And I ask you to be a little bit braver.

00:07:18.422 --> 00:07:23.540
To deal with a little bit of sacrifice and
discomfort so that we don't get hurt?

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:28.487
The truth is I can't trust
you to do that because

00:07:28.487 --> 00:07:33.075
the evidence I've seen in my life so
far is that most people will not stand up.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.989
But I can't think of any framework that
will protect us if you're not willing

00:07:40.989 --> 00:07:44.126
to be brave and
have a backbone for your friends.

00:07:46.618 --> 00:07:51.370
Do this early and often because it is
a muscle that needs to be exercised.

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.820
My own journey with this
began not long after the rape

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:01.180
when I was a baby engineer in
my first real engineering job.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.148
I was sitting at lunch with my coworkers,

00:08:03.148 --> 00:08:05.688
eating the same deli sandwich
I had every single day.

00:08:05.688 --> 00:08:11.650
And we were talking about what
we like to do on the weekends.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:16.797
When my coworker James said, sometimes
what we love to do is just drive around

00:08:16.797 --> 00:08:22.431
town and yell things out the window at
the prostitutes like, hey, you're pretty.

00:08:24.686 --> 00:08:30.307
I heard laughter, and
the sound of the conversation

00:08:30.307 --> 00:08:35.940
moving on as I said nothing and
felt anger and shame.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:43.810
The next day, I sat at lunch,
same people, same sandwich, and

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:47.510
waited, tensed for
an opening in the conversation.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:51.460
And when one didn't come,
I jumped in swinging anyway.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.204
Does he know what it's like for

00:08:55.204 --> 00:08:59.128
a marginalized person to be
yelled at by random men in a car?

00:09:02.137 --> 00:09:07.810
It was awkward and ugly and way too late
and not exactly what I wanted to say.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.447
But still, I felt avenged and

00:09:10.447 --> 00:09:15.945
none of them ever said anything
like that in front of me again.

00:09:18.671 --> 00:09:21.877
I want you to be a Laura or a Joanna.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:27.190
Speak up, no matter how imperfectly.

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:30.290
Live with the discomfort and complexity.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.840
And make sure the people around
you know where you stand.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:38.796
When I heard my coworker say something
that I thought was not right,

00:09:38.796 --> 00:09:40.298
I did what had to be done.

00:09:43.433 --> 00:09:46.801
The question is, what will you do?

00:09:49.769 --> 00:09:52.176
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:52.176 --> 00:09:56.984
[MUSIC]

